course: dictionary
lesson: 11954
title: A carful of words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A carful means as many people or things as a car can hold. You can work out the meaning from the two parts of the word, car and full. «We set off with a carful of children for the football match.» The car is full of children, so a carful is as many children as the car can hold.
explain: Look at the words around it. «She unloaded a carful of shopping in five minutes.» Words like unloaded and shopping tell you the car was packed with bags. Ask yourself: how full is the car? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: A carful can be people or things: a carful of kids, a carful of shopping, a carful of bricks. The word is not very common. Many people say a carload instead, and it means exactly the same thing.
fact: A carful is as many people or things as a car can hold.
fact: «Carful» is made from the two words car and full.
fact: «A carful of children» means a car packed with children.
fact: Some people say «a carload» instead; it means the same thing.
fact: In maths, a carful can be a unit: if one carful holds 5 children, you need 5 cars to take 23 children.
exercise: «We set off with a carful of children for the match.» What is a carful? | A very long road trip | *As many people or things as a car can hold | A bag for shopping | A car that is broken
exercise: Which two smaller words is carful made from? | Car and fly | *Car and full | Carp and foul | Cart and full
exercise: «She unloaded a carful of shopping in five minutes.» Find and copy one word that tells you what was in the car. | minutes | *shopping | five | unloaded
test: A teacher says, «One carful holds 5 children. How many cars do we need for 23 children?» What is the answer? | 6 | 23 | 4 | *5
test: «The shop had lots of toys, but we only had room for a carful.» What does a carful mean here? | A very small box | Two cars full | A bag of toys | *As much as a car can hold
exam: Which phrase means the same as a carful of children? | *A car packed full of children | A child who likes cars | Two bags of children | A school bus with no seats
